 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over WebSocket connections that the project operates itself.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Each committed edit is assigned one event revision by the server, and those revisions are kept durable and replayable. The delivery service subscribes to committed revisions and publishes them to the browser sessions attached to the same event. Browsers apply revisions as they arrive; when a connection drops, the browser reconnects and catches up from the revisions it has not yet seen.
+
+The project owns connection capacity, session lifecycle, and reconnect handling. That operational work is accepted in exchange for update latency measured in subseconds and no recurring realtime vendor bill.
 
